# A Last Testament!
Tamil title: மரண சாசனம்!

“I did not die! I did not roll upon a bed and die after germs pierced my body, drank my blood and reduced me to a living corpse, my sight dimmed, my ears blocked, phlegm gathering as I coughed, hiccupped and sweated. I did not die from the poison of a snakebite or from being mauled by a tiger. I did not die, comrades—I was killed!

“I was killed while joyfully performing my duty. I was killed while labouring enthusiastically for the town's improvement, driven by the fire of conviction blazing in my heart. I worked to make the lives of the people prosper, caring nothing for the ruin of my own life, and I was killed by those very people.

“A bewildered people killed me because they mistook a friend for an enemy, regarded one who served them as a traitor, and allowed ideas sown in their minds by an adversary to grow. They imagined that by killing me they were removing some great evil. They did not know they had killed their own comrade, friend and kinsman.

“My blood will remain before their eyes. Even if only after a long time, they will shed tears upon realising that the blood spilled was a martyr's final service in washing clean the stains upon society's shore. I laboured for them in word and deed; my blood too has now served them.

“I did not perish in vain. I died after doing my work. Those who scorn me exist today; tomorrow they will know the truth. What if they never do? I know that until life left me I worked to spread the noble principles in which I wholeheartedly believed, and died in that very service.

“I did not die. Had I merely died, this testament would have been unnecessary, for I would have worked throughout my natural life. But I was killed. Had I lived, I would have continued working. Because I was killed before completing everything I could do, some of the work remains unfinished. Those who show me compassion and shed tears for me must perform that remaining task on my behalf. This is my last testament.”

Every comrade in the Dravidar Kazhagam must prepare such a testament. We have awakened a fallen people and reached a stage at which freedom must be obtained at any price. Blades and stabbings will now come swiftly in search of us. We shall have to travel upon the swing suspended between life and death.

The disturbance on the banks of the Vaigai last week teaches us precisely that lesson. Varnashrama has understood beyond doubt that our very existence endangers its dominance. Knowing that it can survive for even a little longer only if we die, it staged a savage dance demanding our deaths and tasted a little of our blood.

In Madurai, where the retinues of Pandyan kings once moved and where Yavanas and Greeks scattered gold and gems to buy cloth and fragrant aloes, Tamils hurled stones, earth, knives, sickles and sticks and spilled Tamil blood across the broad riverbed from daylight until night. Men and women were beaten until they screamed. Those who were caught were brutalised. Madurai was thrown into turmoil.

After what did all this occur? After the greatest conference Madurai had ever seen had continued for a day and a night, filling people's hearts with overflowing joy. It occurred after the conference president, Periyar, spent hours insisting that Dravidian comrades needed discipline and order and spoke conciliatorily: “We shall not enter into blind opposition against the Congress government. If it undertakes good measures beneficial to the people, we shall cooperate.” Why, then, did a great riot erupt?

The thought tormented some: “Could such a conference be held in Madurai? Could more than forty thousand people attend? Could women gather in such numbers? Could the procession proceed so beautifully, peacefully and with such discipline? Could propaganda be presented without hurting anyone, explaining issues and cultivating knowledge? We can find no way to arouse riotous feeling. What shall we do? Can crowds remain all night as though at a festival? Can the moon swim in the blue sky, the white sand shine in its light, and Dravidian comrades stand joyfully upon that sand while we tolerate the sight?”

They gathered. We do not know what they discussed or what plan they made. The following day they turned the garden of smiles into a place of violent assault, let rioting dance where joy had walked, and carried out destructive work.

To justify it all, they alleged that women going to the temple had been abused, a flag burned and an idol taken. Who supposedly committed these acts? Members of the Blackshirt Corps. Were there no officials or staff in the temple? Why did they not seize those comrades immediately and hand them to the authorities? Did they do so? No. They neither produced the alleged offenders nor named witnesses to the acts. Officials of the temple where these things allegedly occurred did not utter a word about them.

Although the complaint concerned an affront to the Meenakshi temple, the disturbance was not carried out by praising the goddess. It proceeded amid cries of “Vande Mataram” and nationalist slogans. Can anyone claim there was consistency or intelligible meaning between the two?

After unlawfully creating the disturbance, they fashioned this curtain to conceal their own act. Their only confidence was that those in power belonged to their party and that no one would question whatever they did to opponents or whatever accusation they hurled at them.

Wherever they found members of the Blackshirt Corps, they beat and chased them. They burned black shirts and even burned the conference pavilion. The nationalist newspapers wrote with inward delight that an enraged public had done it. A long-festering resentment in their hearts must have found some relief.

After writing of these events triumphantly, some papers began offering advice. One declared, “The country will tolerate anything, but it will not tolerate interference with temples.” Some wrote in a manner that applauded the deeds and encouraged more. Others came forward to mock us and rejoice that the Madurai incident had enabled the whole province to understand their enormous influence.

For a movement suffering without its own newspaper, all this is free publicity. Even by paying, we could not have informed the people through so many newspapers and All India Radio about the growth of the Blackshirt Corps. We obtained it by giving a little blood.

Though accusations were made and people may for that day, the next and a few more days have believed, “They must have done it,” they will gradually ask: “Could it be true? Would they have acted so? Why would they? Even supposing they did, is it not proper to arrest and punish the offenders? Should thousands be tortured and an entire town engulfed in disorder for their sake?” Every honest person who reflects will come to understand the truth.

The incident occurred in Madurai, but all India now speaks of the Blackshirt Corps. The price we paid was a small quantity of blood. Blood would flow even if one tripped over a stone; it would flow if a motor vehicle struck someone on a pleasure trip. The blood mingled with the sands of the Vaigai has brought us far greater benefit.

The old order—Varnashrama and Aryanism—has revealed its belief that it cannot survive if we live. We thank it for disclosing this.

Madurai offers unusually fertile ground for easily stirring riotous feeling. Careful observers can understand this well. The nationalist papers themselves revealed the truth when they wrote with satisfaction that an “enraged public” had done these things.

Those who say the entire disturbance occurred only because the Blackshirts behaved improperly should examine Madurai's many incidents closely. They will recognise that the place has become suited to such unrest.

Last week anger was directed against the Blackshirt Corps, and the blame was therefore shifted onto it.

What happened this week? A crowd blocked and stopped the Trivandrum Express. The driver and fireman were attacked, carried to a nearby cremation ground and beaten. Their purses and wristwatches were taken. Water was poured into the engine to extinguish it. Alarmed passengers feared greater danger, got down and continued on foot. Reserve Police later arrived and the train proceeded to Madurai under protection.

This was the latest “divine sport” enacted in Madurai. This time the attack was not upon Blackshirts but upon the railway. Although there was a railway strike throughout the country, no such disturbance occurred anywhere except Madurai. The strike leader would hardly have imagined that the strike could be protected by robbing the driver and fireman who opposed it of their purses and watches. Nothing like this happened elsewhere, but it happened in Madurai.

The day before the Blackshirt conference, processions and shop closures were held in many places across the country following the death of Comrade Bhulabhai Desai. There was no disorder elsewhere. In Madurai there was: rioting in the procession and stones thrown at shops. Reserve Police intervened, fired tear gas and dispersed the crowd. Only in Madurai!

Before that, a clash between Congress members and Communists led to a great disturbance in Madurai. Stones were thrown at the Communist office, fierce fighting occurred on both sides and a frightening atmosphere prevailed.

Earlier still, during the dispute between Kamaraj and Rajagopalachari, a protest procession brought riots, stone-throwing, hand-to-hand fighting, stabbings and more.

To those who throw dust in people's eyes by saying that Madurai's public became angry and rioted only because the Blackshirts had done something improper, we say this: such events have become everyday occurrences in Madurai. This “festival” comes every week; a “divine sport” takes place every month.

Nationalist papers publish all those incidents in tiny type in obscure corners. But they joyfully placed the attack upon the Blackshirt Corps on the front page in large letters, hoping thereby to make the nation hate the Blackshirts.

Some among the public read newspaper reports and also put thought to work. They will recognise that riots and disturbances take place in Madurai every week and that any excuse is enough to set disorder dancing. Those among them with firm minds will ask the government why riotous feeling remains so insatiable in Madurai and demand an inquiry into who continually incites it.

The nationalist formula that “the people of Madurai, enraged against the Blackshirt Corps, rioted” is therefore not the whole truth. Some riot takes place in Madurai every week; this week the rioters attacked the Blackshirts.

Another group argues that a great disturbance arose because the Blackshirts did something the public disliked. That argument too is wrong.

Once a group has decided to riot, it commits the riot and seeks a reason afterward. Rioting was directed not only against the Blackshirt Corps but, in the preceding month, against Gandhi as well. To protect their position, nationalist papers call those rioters “hooligans” while calling the Madurai rioters “an enraged public.” With help from their dictionary, they give the same matter a different form.

But a riot remains a riot. It occurs not only against the Blackshirt Corps but against Gandhi too. Comrades who think they mock the Blackshirts by asking, “So there was a riot? Injuries? Stabbings?” will, after calm reflection, understand that riots occur over anything, and this one occurred in the same way.

The attack upon the Blackshirt Corps was therefore incited by Aryanism, tormented by the complete success of the previous day's conference, using a crowd always ready to riot in a Madurai already thick with the atmosphere of unrest. The claims that Blackshirts entered a temple and insulted women were stories devised to prevent others from condemning the attack.

Let us now consider why the riot did not end with mere shouting and showers of stone and earth, but continued for an entire day.

The nationalist papers wrote, truthfully, that “Blackshirts were beaten wherever they were found.” But another truth is contained within that statement: if Blackshirts were beaten wherever they were found, Blackshirts must have been present everywhere. Madurai is not a village but a great city, and members of the Corps were found in every part of it. Comrades had attended the conference in such vast numbers. This itself testifies to its scale.

Because the crowd was so large, the rioters could not complete their work quickly or easily. A few years earlier, a single cry of “Vande Mataram” would have ended the matter: the conference would disperse and no crowd would remain. Today conditions are different. The conference drew a multitude committed to a principle. Mere shouting could not scatter it. Stones were not enough; knives and sickles were needed. A small band could not accomplish the task; the entire town had to be mobilised. One or two hours did not suffice; a whole day was required. What is the deeper meaning?

We have grown to such an extent that in Madurai Aryanism had to assemble its whole strength and oppose us with all its breath for an entire day.

Even this opposition did not arise after our programme and principles had been refuted and the people persuaded to dislike them. Opposition could be organised only by burdening us with accusations calculated to alarm the public and then gathering the town. They argued against our principles and found no success. They consequently reached the point of saying we had done what we had not done, in the hope of inciting the people. This is a sign of our growth.

The Brahmin Seva Sangh too recognises it. Its petition is a valuable testimonial to our growth.

Some think: all that may be true, but such disturbances cause damage and blood is wasted. To them, one word: consider for a moment the nature of the great undertaking we have accepted.

We seek to reform arrangements said to have existed for age upon age. We are placing explosives against the mountain of arrogance. If small fragments scatter and split our skulls, it means the charge we set is breaking the mountain. Having undertaken to split a mountain, can we expect flowers to fall upon our heads?

We have dedicated ourselves to this work. The suffering of those who undertook comparable work in many parts of the world has now become material in university textbooks. The poison Socrates drank then has given him immortality to this day.

We belong to the lineage of those who took blame, degradation, opposition and danger upon their heads, continued their work, and enabled later generations to enjoy its fruits. We shall certainly face suffering and obstruction, but our labour will never be wasted.

The lives of those engaged in social revolution read alike: poisoned to death; confined for life in dark prisons; stoned and driven away; nailed to crosses; fed to leopards; strangled; cast into fire; cut into pieces; chased and beaten by their own people; left without support. We are of that kind. Today they are the ornaments of the intellectual world. Posterity will not forget us either.

There were ages when molten lead was poured into ears, people were bound inside houses and burned, thrown into wells and covered with stones, their eyes gouged out, tongues cut, bodies impaled, heads severed and bodies cast into flames. Those who spoke for reform suffered these things. Most did not merely die; they were killed. Because they were killed, they remain undying today.

That is why we say we must prepare a last testament before entering this great struggle.

If we, who desire fundamental change in society, abandon the work because we might be killed, death itself will not take a rest. Even if we preach peace, the hymn will still sound in our ears: “Alas, there is no certainty that those present today will remain tomorrow!”

If one dies, one is dead. But if one is killed while performing duty, one gains immortality. We shall live in the speech and songs of our descendants, who gain a new life through the fruit of our labour. Beside cradles, near flower gardens, by beds and in debating halls, people will say of us, “They worked for us and were killed.” That will mean we are alive.

When death comes, are we troubled by thoughts of what will happen to a mansion or lovely women, the fate of gardens and fields, who will inherit earrings and pendants, whether trade will decline or interest income be lost? No.

We shall die saying: “Aryanism! You have killed me. You did so because you knew my life endangered you. Do not be satisfied. Turn and look! Many others remain who are not corpses.” Therefore prepare your last testament. That is Madurai's great lesson to us.

The day after Madurai, many thousands of our comrades gathered at Kumbakonam. There were even more mothers there than in Madurai. Two days of conferences and two nights of plays, and the scale and nature of the awakening, could make the Madurai incident seem a vision seen in a dream.

What does this mean? Our comrades demonstrated that the Madurai incident would not dim our great movement. Thousands of enthusiastic and impassioned young people gathered there, some bearing scars from Madurai, and proclaimed themselves ready to work.

Madurai had fallen quiet. The pain in the hands of those who threw stones at our comrades may not yet have subsided, but before their wounds from stones and knives had healed, our comrades gathered at Kumbakonam to work as always. Yes, they have entered this great undertaking after preparing their last testaments.

We praise their courage, spirit of sacrifice and resolve. We bow our heads to their bravery.

Comrades who shed blood upon the sand, your blood was not spilled in vain. That news has made the blood of countless Tamils boil. It has given them a frame of mind that sees no distinction between life and death. It has planted the thought: “We may be killed; therefore, while we remain today, let us do a little more work for the awakening of our people.”

The Madurai incident has swept away even what little attachment to family and kin remained among our people. All that remains now is ourselves, our service, the Aryanism that trembles upon seeing it, the weapons it launches, and the urgent need to work while facing them. That is all. We have prepared our last testaments and entered this great struggle.

*Dravida Nadu*, 26 May 1946
